Based on my analysis, the endpoint inherits `UserEndpoint` which enforces `UserPermission` (requester must be the target user, system auth, or active superuser) via `convert_args` → `check_object_permissions`. Both GET and PUT scope all reads/writes to `user_id=user.id` (the resolved, permission-checked user), so there is no IDOR. The PUT writes `scope_identifier`/`scope_type` from user input without verifying the requester is a member of that organization/team, but the existing `validate_scope` helper (which enforces membership) is not part of this section's changed code and no explicit policy rule mandates it, so I cannot confirm a policy violation. No confirmed authorization flaw is introduced by the changed lines.

